# Prose Style

Write clear, direct prose. Subject first. Plain verbs. No flowery language. No ornamental description for mood.

## Detail

- Aim for solid coverage of the beat. Too sparse is wrong. Padding is also wrong.
- Give enough detail to track what happened and what he did. Do not add justifying color.
- No extraneous explanation. Do not tell why a fact matters unless the plot needs it.
- No comparisons used as flavor.
- Bad padding: "the room was already warm" "potatoes that did not look soft" "before the day got hotter" and similar extra judgments.
- When rewriting a line or beat, keep equal or better coverage. Do not delete content and leave a thinner stub. Replace bad motive or tone with better wording of the same beat length and detail.

## Sentences

- Keep wording straightforward. Say who did what and when.
- Prefer subject-first lines like "D woke up when the sunlight hit his eyes."
- Do not invert into sensory-first or stylish openings like "Light cut across his eyes so he sat up."
- Do not swap plain words for prettier ones. Prefer "hit" over "cut across." Prefer "woke up" over ornamental wake phrasing.
- Never end a beat with a two-word sentence. "He paid." "He woke." "He waited." are banned.
- A subject-verb line is fine when it carries real attached content: object, place, time or linked next step.
- Prefer short to medium sentences. Long sentences are rare and usually only when the user writes them that way on purpose.

```
# Bad (flowery / inverted)
Light cut across his eyes so he sat up.

# Bad (bare two-word beat)
D woke. He paid.

# Bad (padded)
D woke up when the sunlight hit his eyes and the room was already warm.

# Good
D woke up when the sunlight hit his eyes. He sat up, checked that he had cash and a bag and walked to the market two streets over. The shelves were half stocked so he took bread, eggs, milk and a sack of potatoes. He paid for the groceries and then carried them home.
```

## Point of View

- Use third person.
- When in the main character's point of view, stay close to what he knows, notices, wants and decides.
- Do not drift into other characters' private thoughts during his POV scenes.
- Narration can report what he observes about others but not what they feel unless he has reason to infer it.

## Tone

- Concrete over poetic.
- Functional over pretty.
- Clarity over style tricks.
- Straightforward subject-first beats over rearranged "literary" order.
